# Every claim that spans a width, beside whether it claims to have none. One row per ranged claim
# anywhere in a document, `violates` where narrowsWhen is `notApplicable`.
#
# The other half of checks/narrows_a_point_value.sqlc
#   The pair is why both are worth having. `notApplicable` on a narrowing means "there is no range
#   here to narrow"; filed against a claim that plainly spans one, it is a category error the
#   document can see for itself.
#
# Every claim, not only demands
#   `every-local-part` files two eliminations under the same reasoning: one of 1800, 2000 and 2400,
#   and one that is a single value, 2160 exactly. One document, two claims, and only the width
#   tells them apart: the single value answers `notApplicable` and the range answers `unmeasured`,
#   which is what that filing's own source demand says. Confined to the demand columns, this rule
#   could not reach the ranged one; over every claim it does.
#
# The answer that passes, for a ranged elimination
#   It is in the corpus and worth copying: `instrument`, with the measurement that would tighten
#   it, over a `none` bound origin: "nothing sets this bound. It is the size of the double counting
#   the composer found".
-- pm:Claim/pm:narrowsWhen with pm:absent/reason = notApplicable on any claim where low <> high.
SELECT r.rule, p.filing, p.layer, p.violates, p.detail
FROM      (
    :compose(checks/roster.sqlc)
) r
LEFT JOIN (
    SELECT c.filing, c.layer,
           c.narrows_absent IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'notApplicable' AS violates,
           format('%s claim %s: spans %s to %s %s, yet says there is no range to narrow',
                  c.owns, c.seq, c.low, c.high, c.unit) AS detail
    FROM (
        :compose(epistemics/ranged_claims.sqlc)
    ) c
) p ON true
WHERE r.slug = 'range_says_no_range'
